  "query_preview": "..\n\nA rex block continues to drift over the eastern Great Lakes today \nwith broad surface high pressure anchored over southern Quebec \nproviding strongly subsident and stable conditions. Easterly wind \npersists through the day but with slightly lower magnitude than \nyesterday given a weakening gradient. This advects a drier air mass \nin with PWAT slated to fall below 0.50\", bringing lots of sunshine. \nHigh temperatures are forecast to land within a few degrees on either\nside of 70 degrees. Another clear, crisp fall evening is in store \nwith lows tonight falling well into the 40s outside of the urban \ncore.\n\nBy Friday the rex block evolves into a coastal low but leaves \nlongwave ridging in control of the Great Lakes. A weakening upper \nshortwave brings increasing coverage of high clouds late tonight \ninto Friday which dampens high temps a few degrees compared to \ntoday, but highly stable and dry conditions in the mid and low \nlevels ensure dry weather. As the coastal low drifts northward \ntoward New England this weekend, deep layer subsidence settles back \nin across SE Michigan. This favors mainly clear skies and light wind \nwith high pressure established overhead. A good amount of sun and \nair mass modification will facilitate a gradual warming trend \nthrough the weekend. This stagnant patterns looks to hold through \nMonday before the coastal low finally begins to release east.\n\nThe next opportunity for more active weather targets the local area \nbetween late Tuesday and Thursday as a Pacific wave treks through. \nThere are a subset of solutions that carve out a cutoff low over the \nFour Corners, leaving a disorganized frontal system to track through \nthe Great Lakes. Other solutions carry a more amplified 500mb wave \ndirectly across the region, producing a more dynamic surface low \nwith potential for thunder and/or stronger wind. For now, a chance \nPoP will be advertised for this broader mid-week period until \ndetails come into focus.",
